I’ll inspect the specimen metadata, pattern, frozen index, and the supplied refinement workflow first. Then I’ll build and compare phase models, refine the accepted mixture in GSAS-II, run the required bounded polish once, and export every deliverable from that exact final project.

The specimen is the 800 °C WO₃–Mn₃O₄ system, with a broad but bounded synthetic-lab instrument/specimen model. I’m now interrogating the frozen reference index and extracting coherent peak families; that will distinguish ordinary Mn–W–O products/precursors before any analogue adaptation is considered.

The dominant reflection family matches monoclinic wolframite MnWO₄ line-for-line (including the diagnostic 30.0/30.4° doublet). A second family is consistent with monoclinic WO₃, and weak residuals near the tetragonal Mn₃O₄ lines suggest unreacted precursor. I’m putting those ordinary phases through an equal-stage Rietveld checkpoint now; any coherent residual left after that will drive the missing-topology search.

The ordinary three-phase checkpoint confirms MnWO₄ and WO₃ at substantial positive scale, but the Mn₃O₄ start is not physically stable: its tetragonal axis drifts about 2%, far beyond the supplied reference-mismatch range. I’m treating that as a rejected basin, not as phase evidence. I also caught and am correcting two exploratory setup defects (an unconstrained histogram scale and misaddressed profile bounds) before interpreting residuals quantitatively.

The large apparent Rwp gains in the first exploratory runs did not replay: they came from variables frozen at tested bounds, so I have discarded those numerical checkpoints. The reliable conclusion so far is structural—MnWO₄ and monoclinic WO₃ are secure, while exact Mn₃O₄ is only a weak, unstable explanation. I’m now comparing the chemically balanced Mn₂WO₆ intermediate in two distinct parent topologies (orthorhombic and trirutile), as required for a genuinely unassigned residual family.

