The symmetry comparison has resolved the MnO-like family: hexagonal MnO gives the best replay-stable fit with a physically small cell shift, while both cubic starts fit worse. I’m now indexing the remaining low-angle residual family and testing whether it is a genuine fourth phase or unresolved line-shape/intensity structure from the accepted phases.

The residual screening is narrowing the possibilities. The apparent high-angle “extra” peaks mostly coincide with weak reflections or Kα₂ components of MnWO₄/WO₃, so they are not independent evidence for another phase. The genuinely independent set is much smaller—chiefly near 19.85°, 25.42°, 31.76°, 32.48°, 35.11°, and 46.49°—and I’m indexing that set rather than allowing profile broadening to absorb it.

The indexed residual is consistent with an ordered Pbcn Mn₂WO₆-type superstructure adapted from Fe₂WO₆: the ordered start remains at about 4–5 wt% and fits substantially better than the compositionally identical disordered Pbcn start. I’m now checking whether the MnO evidence requires one polymorph or both; this is the last inventory decision before full profile/coordinate refinement.

The phase inventory is now physically closed at five phases: MnWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, cubic MnO, hexagonal MnO, and an ordered Pbcn Mn₂WO₆-type phase. I’m finishing the supported coordinate and line-profile terms now; the remaining work is refinement stability, the single mandated polish pass, and exact-project export.

The coordinate tests are separating cleanly: MnWO₄ oxygen positions and the ordered Mn₂WO₆ tungsten site improve the fit without hitting bounds. The hexagonal MnO oxygen coordinate is weak and is being treated cautiously because its origin-related correlation makes it less reliable.

The phasewise broadening test is finding that several “global” width refinements fail by pinning at limits, exactly as the earlier all-phase trial suggested. I’m retaining only phase-specific size/strain values that converge in the interior; limit-pinned terms will be rolled back instead of being mistaken for evidence.

The broadening model has reached a defensible branch: MnWO₄ is best represented with no microstrain and a refined ~0.255 µm size; WO₃ needs stable uniaxial strain; Mn₂WO₆ supports an interior isotropic strain term. Cubic and hexagonal MnO width refinements run to limits and are being discarded, so their small fractions will carry larger uncertainty. Next I’m checking texture axes phase-by-phase.

